The function is **`extractJsonObject(raw: string): unknown`**, defined in `packages/core/src/prompts/index.ts` immediately after the `JSON_FENCE` constant:

```ts
const JSON_FENCE = /```(?:json)?[ \t]*\r?\n([\s\S]*?)```/;

/** Models wrap JSON in fences and prose; tolerate that, but tolerate nothing else. */
function extractJsonObject(raw: string): unknown {
  const candidates = [raw.trim()];
  const fenced = JSON_FENCE.exec(raw);
  if (fenced?.[1] !== undefined) candidates.push(fenced[1]);
  const open = raw.indexOf('{');
  const close = raw.lastIndexOf('}');
  if (open >= 0 && close > open) candidates.push(raw.slice(open, close + 1));

  for (const candidate of candidates) {
    try {
      return JSON.parse(candidate);
    } catch {
      // Next shape.
    }
  }
  throw new SummaryContractError('response: no JSON object found in the model reply');
}
```

It tries three candidate shapes in order — the raw trimmed string, a fenced ```json block, and the span from the first `{` to the last `}` — and throws the `SummaryContractError` ("no JSON object found in the model reply") if none parse. That error message is the one surfacing in the FM-1 §8 contract failures in the iter logs.

Worth noting: there's a second, near-identical `JSON_FENCE` constant in `packages/core/src/summarize/contract.ts`, where the analogous function is `asJsonObject(raw)` (used by `dropTreeOwnedContents` before `parseSummaryReply`). The difference in contract: `extractJsonObject` throws on a miss, while `asJsonObject` returns `null` on a miss and hands the raw text back for the strict parser to diagnose.
